In fact, most consider that unless you always make a practice of
deleting all your bones files as soon as they're created, it's not fair
to suddenly decide to now, whether or not you do delete all the files
that are currently in your directory.

If there's a particularly crazy bones file, you could always get to the
town, figure out whether it's the bones file, and then go up a
staircase and never return in that game if it is; this way it gets rid
of the bones file (since it gets loaded up and you don't die on that
level).  In the case of minetown, you can get along without ever
spending time there, but if you end up with some incredibly hard level
in the main dungeon you could always just go up onto a different level,
then #quit (dying on purpose would be bones-stuffing, and you don't
want to avoid one dishonest method by using another...).

However, when I have tough bones files around I usually see them as
more of a bonus than anything else.  In your case, for instance, I'd
just hang around in the main dungeon or the upper mines levels and not
go into minetown until I was pretty sure that I could handle the
warhorse and whatever else I'd left there.  If you want to be a jerk,
you could always use the knowledge that there're tough monsters there
and play monks (who're likely to be able to cast Sleep) or some other
class that's similarly well-suited to beating up on tough monsters
early on.

Good luck with whatever you do about it!

